1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,529
Fellow: an employee management app, and the DevOps pipeline that ships it.

2
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:10,526
Fellow covers people, departments, leave, attendance and holidays for a small company.

3
00:00:10,526 --> 00:00:15,351
The stack: React and FastAPI, tested, containerised, deployed to Kubernetes and monitored.

4
00:00:15,483 --> 00:00:18,735
The README documents the whole project: features, architecture, setup and operations.

5
00:00:18,735 --> 00:00:27,161
Features: each area of the app and the business rules it enforces.

6
00:00:27,161 --> 00:00:34,593
Architecture: the ingress sends / to the React frontend and /api to the FastAPI backend.

7
00:00:34,593 --> 00:00:40,097
Delivery: tests, image build, Trivy scan, push to GHCR, then Argo CD deploys from Git.

8
00:00:40,097 --> 00:00:48,721
The tech stack, layer by layer.

9
00:00:48,867 --> 00:00:52,620
Part 1: the application.

10
00:00:52,783 --> 00:00:58,768
The dashboard shows live numbers from the FastAPI backend, which queries PostgreSQL.

11
00:00:58,768 --> 00:01:05,128
The sidebar collapses to an icon rail, and the choice is remembered in the browser.

12
00:01:05,128 --> 00:01:15,094
Employees can be searched and filtered, and every page has its own URL.

13
00:01:15,094 --> 00:01:22,243
A profile shows personal and job details, leave balances and monthly attendance.

14
00:01:22,243 --> 00:01:32,535
Approving a leave request updates the request and the employee's balance through the API.

15
00:01:32,535 --> 00:01:41,528
Checking in and out uses the server's clock, so the time cannot be changed from the browser.

16
00:01:41,528 --> 00:01:45,714
Light and dark themes are both supported.

17
00:01:45,867 --> 00:01:51,600
Architecture: browser, ingress, frontend and backend pods, PostgreSQL, Prometheus, Grafana and Argo CD.

18
00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:55,504
Part 2: the codebase.

19
00:01:55,667 --> 00:01:59,436
One repository holds the app, the pipeline, the infrastructure and the docs.

20
00:01:59,436 --> 00:02:08,252
The backend is split into FastAPI routers, one file per resource.

21
00:02:08,252 --> 00:02:16,874
Routers validate input with Pydantic schemas and use SQLAlchemy to talk to PostgreSQL.

22
00:02:16,874 --> 00:02:29,846
The CI/CD workflow lives in .github/workflows/ci-cd.yml.

23
00:02:29,846 --> 00:02:37,728
The Helm chart templates describe every Kubernetes object the app needs.

24
00:02:37,883 --> 00:02:42,133
FastAPI generates this interactive OpenAPI documentation automatically, at /docs.

25
00:02:42,133 --> 00:02:47,455
Every endpoint is listed with its method, path, parameters and response schema.

26
00:02:47,455 --> 00:02:58,792
Try it out: GET /api/employees/12 returns that employee as JSON, with status 200.

27
00:02:58,792 --> 00:03:04,944
The response: status 200 with the employee as JSON, plus the matching curl command.

28
00:03:04,944 --> 00:03:17,987
Next, an invalid update: PUT with the email set to null.

29
00:03:17,987 --> 00:03:26,009
The API rejects it with status 422 and a clear message: email cannot be null.

30
00:03:26,009 --> 00:03:31,431
The Schemas section lists every request and response model, defined with Pydantic.

31
00:03:31,599 --> 00:03:35,373
Part 3: CI/CD.

32
00:03:35,516 --> 00:03:41,241
Every push to main runs the pipeline: test, build, scan, publish, promote and sync.

33
00:03:41,399 --> 00:03:50,644
A real change is committed and pushed to main from the terminal.

34
00:03:50,644 --> 00:03:59,888
The push triggers the GitHub Actions workflow.

35
00:04:00,049 --> 00:04:07,536
pytest and the frontend build run first; a failing test stops the pipeline (sped up 8x).

36
00:04:07,536 --> 00:04:15,022
Both Docker images are built and scanned by Trivy, which fails the build on HIGH or CRITICAL CVEs.

37
00:04:15,022 --> 00:04:22,508
Images are pushed to GitHub Container Registry, tagged with the commit SHA.

38
00:04:22,508 --> 00:04:29,995
The last job commits the new image tag to Git for the staging environment.

39
00:04:29,995 --> 00:04:37,481
Both images are listed in GitHub Container Registry.

40
00:04:37,633 --> 00:04:49,949
Argo CD watches the Git repository and deploys that commit to the staging namespace.

41
00:04:49,949 --> 00:04:53,223
Synced means the cluster matches Git; Healthy means every resource is running.

42
00:04:53,383 --> 00:04:57,396
Part 4: infrastructure as code with Terraform.

43
00:04:57,566 --> 00:05:03,339
Terraform defines the VPC, subnets, NAT gateway, an optional EKS cluster and the kind cluster.

44
00:05:03,483 --> 00:05:14,071
terraform plan compares the code with what exists and shows what would change.

45
00:05:14,071 --> 00:05:18,429
The outputs list the IDs of the VPC, subnets and NAT gateway created in LocalStack.

46
00:05:18,429 --> 00:05:31,121
The subnets exist in the emulated AWS API: two public and two private, across two zones.

47
00:05:31,121 --> 00:05:43,305
Setting create_eks=true plans the EKS cluster, IAM roles and node group from the same code.

48
00:05:43,466 --> 00:05:47,227
Part 5: Kubernetes and Helm.

49
00:05:47,383 --> 00:05:53,148
One Helm chart deploys both environments: dev with Helm, staging with Argo CD.

50
00:05:53,299 --> 00:05:57,409
The cluster has three nodes: one control plane and two workers.

51
00:05:57,409 --> 00:06:04,729
Backend and frontend run two replicas each behind ClusterIP services; the ingress routes traffic.

52
00:06:04,729 --> 00:06:09,979
helm list shows the releases for the app and for monitoring.

53
00:06:09,979 --> 00:06:32,105
A load generator sends traffic to the backend while the HPA watches CPU (sped up 6x).

54
00:06:32,105 --> 00:06:41,277
CPU goes above the 60% target, so the HPA scales the backend from 2 to 6 pods.

55
00:06:41,449 --> 00:06:45,263
Part 6: observability.

56
00:06:45,399 --> 00:06:50,728
Prometheus collects the metrics and Grafana turns them into a dashboard.

57
00:06:50,883 --> 00:06:54,635
The backend exposes Prometheus metrics at /metrics: request counts by endpoint, method and status.

58
00:06:54,635 --> 00:07:02,352
The PUT 4xx counter is the invalid request sent from the API docs a moment ago.

59
00:07:02,533 --> 00:07:06,902
Prometheus scrapes every backend pod every 15 seconds, and all targets are UP.

60
00:07:06,902 --> 00:07:12,570
This Grafana dashboard ships inside the Helm chart.

61
00:07:12,570 --> 00:07:20,153
During the load test, request rate, latency and CPU per pod all rise.

62
00:07:20,153 --> 00:07:26,217
The autoscaling panel shows the replicas going up to 6.

63
00:07:26,383 --> 00:07:30,142
Part 7: troubleshooting.

64
00:07:30,299 --> 00:07:35,530
Real problems found and fixed while building the project.

65
00:07:35,683 --> 00:07:55,955
Lab 1: a deployment points at an image tag that does not exist.

66
00:07:55,955 --> 00:07:56,760
The pod cannot start: ErrImagePull, then ImagePullBackOff.

67
00:07:56,760 --> 00:08:06,357
The events explain why: the image was not found in the registry.

68
00:08:06,357 --> 00:08:16,839
Lab 2: a Service whose selector matches no pods has no endpoints.

69
00:08:16,839 --> 00:08:32,772
Patching the selector to the real pod label brings the endpoints back.

70
00:08:32,772 --> 00:08:37,728
Both labs are deleted afterwards.

71
00:08:37,866 --> 00:08:43,490
Key learnings: gate early, let Git drive deployments, and measure before scaling.

72
00:08:43,490 --> 00:08:49,113
Code, docs, slides and this video are all in the GitHub repository.

73
00:08:49,283 --> 00:08:55,029
Thank you for watching.

